 "▼ Dive in: <0>pass@k</0>": "",
 "Samples n": "",
 "Correct c": "",
 "Tries k": "",
 "single-run success ĉ/n = {#} / {#} = <0>{#}</0><1/>pass@{#} = {#} − C(n−c, k) / C(n, k) = <2>{#}</2><3/>pass^{#} ≈ (c/n)^k = <4>{#}</4>": "",
 "pass@k as k grows <0/> <1/>": "",
 "<0>pass@k</0> asks \"does any of k attempts succeed?\" (fine if a human picks the best). <1>pass^k</1> asks \"do all k succeed?\" (what an unattended agent needs). Same agent, very different numbers.": "",
 "<0>Illustrative values.</0> n and c are values you set; in practice they come from running one task n times.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>LLM-as-judge</0>": "",
 "A second model call grades the output against a <0>rubric</0>. Input <1/> <2/> → output <3/> <4/>.": "",
 "<0>Judge biases (research suggests, effects vary by model):</0> <1>position</1> (prefers the first answer), <2>verbosity</2> (prefers longer), <3>self-preference</3> (prefers its own style). Mitigate: randomise order, ask for reasons before scores, use a different model family, and <4>spot-check against human labels</4>.": "",
 "Where possible, back judge scores with hard checks: here, \"do the tests pass?\" is a more reliable grader than any rubric.": "",
