 "The loop runs left→right, row by row, then returns to the context builder. Every block links to its section.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>{#} things</0>": "",
 "Every Part II concept hangs off one loop: build context → predict tokens → parse a tool call → run it → append the result → repeat.": "",
 "Click any block in the diagram to revisit that section.": "",
 "Frameworks (LangChain, LangGraph) and protocols (MCP, skills) package parts of this loop; they do not change its shape.": "",
 "Next: the appendix has a glossary and a quiz covering both parts.": "",
 "An agent is a loop around repeated next-token prediction.": "",
 "Tool calls are just JSON <0>tokens</0> the model predicts; the <1>harness</1> is ordinary code that parses them, runs the tool and appends the result. See <2>the agent loop</2>.": "",
 "Everything is one token sequence.": "",
 "System prompt, tool schemas, history, retrieved chunks and tool results are serialized into the same context; memory and RAG are ways of choosing what goes in (<0>memory</0>).": "",
 "Start simple, add agency only when needed.": "",
 "Workflows beat agents on cost and predictability; multi-agent multiplies tokens (<0>patterns</0>).": "",
 "Errors compound and inputs are untrusted.": "",
 "pⁿ shrinks long runs; injected text can steer the model. Use verification, least privilege and approval gates.": "",
 "If you did not measure it, you do not know.": "",
 "Build evals, read traces, run tasks several times (pass@k vs pass^k), and hedge judge-model scores (<0>evals</0>).": "",
 "The same loop as the model sees it: <0>one growing token sequence</0> (colours = who wrote it).": "",
